WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='768f8701-2b7a-5d07-bed8-52a02bf60be9' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '57702ff5-52bc-522c-9718-f67892447e52','Microdynamic compression','Compression aimed at the microdynamics of a signal, that is the short-term, moment-to-moment level variations such as transients and note-to-note differences, as opposed to macrodynamics, the long-term changes between sections. Fast attack and release times let the compressor act on these small, rapid fluctuations, altering perceived punch, density and detail.','Microdynamics are the tiny, fast loud-soft changes in a sound, like hit-to-hit drum detail. This compression works on those small moves, not the slow ones. Fast settings let it react to each little peak.','It exists to shape the fine internal detail of a sound, tightening or emphasising transients and evening out small level differences without necessarily changing the long-term dynamic shape.','An engineer uses fast attack and release settings on a compressor to control note-to-note level jumps in a bass or the hit-to-hit consistency of a drum performance.','Compression',ARRAY['Macrodynamic compression','Transient','Attack time','Release time','Downward compression','Punch']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing microdynamics with macrodynamics and choosing slow settings that miss the fast detail entirely.','Using very fast attack that flattens transients when the goal was to keep them punchy.','UNSAFE: Squeezing microdynamics raises average loudness, which tempts louder monitoring; observe NIOSH/OSHA exposure limits to protect hearing.']::text[],ARRAY['Evening out a fingered bass line so quiet and loud notes match.','Tightening the hit-to-hit level of a snare in a busy groove.','Adding density to a rhythm guitar without squashing its overall arc.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='57702ff5-52bc-522c-9718-f67892447e52' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Microdynamic compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '57702ff5-52bc-522c-9718-f67892447e52','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='57702ff5-52bc-522c-9718-f67892447e52' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '477c8f9f-7310-5233-8aa3-10c187bc8639','Motown compression','An informal term for the dense, heavily and consistently compressed sound associated with classic Motown-era recordings, in which strong dynamic control kept vocals and rhythm elements loud, even and up-front. The aesthetic was shaped to translate on the small mono radio and car speakers of the era, favouring apparent loudness and consistency over wide dynamic range.','Motown records were squeezed so everything stayed loud and steady. That kept the sound punchy on tiny car and radio speakers. Naming a compression style Motown points to that dense, even, up-front feel.','As a descriptive style label, it tells an engineer the goal is a loud, consistent, forward sound with tight dynamics, rather than one specific device or setting.','An engineer chasing a vintage pop feel applies firm, consistent compression to the vocals and rhythm section so the mix stays dense and even on small speakers.','Compression',ARRAY['Downward compression','Parallel compression','Limiting','Makeup gain','Apparent loudness','Mono compatibility']::text[],ARRAY['Treating it as one precise recipe of settings rather than a broad tonal and dynamic aesthetic.','Over-compressing until the mix sounds lifeless, mistaking loss of life for the target density.','UNSAFE: Judging this loud, dense style at high monitor levels for long stretches risks hearing damage; follow NIOSH/OSHA exposure limits.']::text[],ARRAY['Producing a retro-soul track that needs a vintage, up-front feel.','Making a dense mix hold together on phone and laptop speakers.','Referencing classic 1960s pop dynamics during a mix.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='477c8f9f-7310-5233-8aa3-10c187bc8639' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Motown compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '477c8f9f-7310-5233-8aa3-10c187bc8639','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='477c8f9f-7310-5233-8aa3-10c187bc8639' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '4aa2c2fe-3f5c-5dcf-8e18-bae5cded47b1','Mud','A subjective description of a mix or sound that lacks clarity and definition because of an excess or build-up of energy in the low-mid frequencies, roughly the 100 Hz to 500 Hz region. Mud typically arises when many instruments share and reinforce this range, masking detail and making the result sound thick and indistinct.','Mud is when a mix sounds thick and unclear, like everything is smeared together. It usually comes from too much low-mid energy where many instruments pile up. Cutting some of that range clears it.','The term names a common tonal problem so engineers can diagnose and treat it, usually by cutting low-mid build-up or reducing overlap between instruments in that range.','Hearing a thick, unclear mix, an engineer sweeps an equalizer through the low-mids on guitars, keys and room mics to find and reduce the frequencies causing the build-up.','Tonal balance',ARRAY['Low-mids','Masking-release processing','Equalization','Boominess','Clarity','High-pass filter']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming mud lives in the sub-bass and cutting very low, when the problem usually sits higher, in the low-mids.','Fixing mud only on soloed tracks, where the cumulative build-up across the whole mix is not audible.','Cutting so much low-mid that the mix becomes thin and lifeless instead of clear.']::text[],ARRAY['Cleaning up a dense rock mix where multiple guitars crowd the low-mids.','Reducing thickness on a vocal recorded in an untreated room.','Tightening a mix that sounds fine loud but smears on small speakers.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='4aa2c2fe-3f5c-5dcf-8e18-bae5cded47b1' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Mud'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '4aa2c2fe-3f5c-5dcf-8e18-bae5cded47b1','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='4aa2c2fe-3f5c-5dcf-8e18-bae5cded47b1' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'b3dcfa3a-b6ca-5229-8d83-e4bbb478d3bb','Multi-stage release','A compressor release characteristic that uses more than one time constant rather than a single fixed rate: gain typically recovers quickly at first and then more slowly, or switches between rates depending on how long and how hard the signal has driven the detector. Multi-stage release lets the compressor recover naturally from brief transients while avoiding audible pumping on sustained material.','After a compressor turns a sound down, release is how it lets go. A multi-stage release lets go in steps, fast then slow. This handles quick hits and long notes more smoothly.','It exists to make gain recovery sound natural across varied material, combining a fast stage that follows transients with a slower stage that prevents pumping and low-frequency distortion.','An engineer selects a program-dependent or multi-stage release, often an ''auto'' mode, on a bus compressor so it recovers cleanly on both sharp drum hits and sustained pads.','Compression',ARRAY['Release time','Attack time','Program-dependent release','Auto release','Pumping','Multistage compression']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming release is always a single fixed time and fighting pumping that a multi-stage or auto mode would solve.','Setting the fast stage so quick that low-frequency content distorts as the gain modulates within each cycle.','Confusing multi-stage release with multistage compression, which chains several compressors rather than shaping one recovery curve.']::text[],ARRAY['Using a bus compressor''s auto-release to glue a full mix without pumping.','Controlling a dynamic vocal that has both fast consonants and long sustained notes.','Taming a drum bus so it recovers fast on hits but stays smooth between them.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='b3dcfa3a-b6ca-5229-8d83-e4bbb478d3bb' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Multi-stage release'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'b3dcfa3a-b6ca-5229-8d83-e4bbb478d3bb','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='b3dcfa3a-b6ca-5229-8d83-e4bbb478d3bb' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '99144fc2-9e77-53dd-b788-616820a3c218','Multistage compression','A dynamics approach in which two or more compressors are used in series so that the total gain reduction is shared across stages, each stage handling part of the task, for example one catching fast peaks at a high ratio and another applying gentle overall level control. Spreading the work reduces the audible side effects that a single compressor doing all the reduction would produce.','Instead of one compressor squeezing hard, you use several in a row. Each one does a little of the work. Sharing the job sounds smoother than making one do it all.','It exists to achieve strong yet transparent dynamic control by dividing the reduction among stages, so no single compressor is pushed into obvious pumping or distortion.','An engineer places a fast peak controller and a slower leveling compressor in series on a vocal, letting each stage handle a different part of the dynamic range.','Compression',ARRAY['Serial compression','Downward compression','Gain reduction','Multi-stage release','Limiting','Bus compression']::text[],ARRAY['Setting every stage to heavy reduction, so the cumulative effect over-compresses and dulls the sound.','Not assigning each stage a distinct job, so the compressors fight rather than share the work.','UNSAFE: Heavy multistage compression raises average level, encouraging louder monitoring; keep within NIOSH/OSHA exposure limits to protect hearing.']::text[],ARRAY['Controlling a wide-dynamic lead vocal with a peak stage plus a leveling stage.','Bus compression where one compressor glues the mix and another catches peaks.','Broadcast or mastering chains that share reduction across several processors.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='99144fc2-9e77-53dd-b788-616820a3c218' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Multistage compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '99144fc2-9e77-53dd-b788-616820a3c218','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='99144fc2-9e77-53dd-b788-616820a3c218' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '6f817fa4-116c-588d-962b-ef93131a867f','Multistage saturation','Harmonic saturation introduced across two or more successive gain stages rather than at a single point, so each stage contributes a modest amount of distortion that accumulates into the overall character. This mirrors analogue signal paths, such as consoles or tape machines, where multiple stages each add harmonics, and it can sound richer or more complex than saturation from one stage.','Saturation adds gentle, warm distortion. Multistage saturation spreads it over several steps in a row. Each step adds a little, building a fuller, more complex tone than one step alone.','It exists to build harmonic richness and ''analogue'' complexity gradually, distributing distortion across stages so no single stage is pushed into harshness.','An engineer drives several saturation stages lightly across a channel or bus, emulating a console-plus-tape path, rather than pushing one processor hard.','Saturation',ARRAY['Saturation','Harmonic distortion','Tape saturation','Gain staging','Multistage compression','Warmth']::text[],ARRAY['Driving every stage hard so the accumulated distortion becomes harsh and fizzy instead of warm.','Ignoring gain staging between stages, letting levels run away and clip.','Assuming more stages always sound better, when the added harmonics can clutter a busy mix.']::text[],ARRAY['Emulating a console-and-tape signal path for analogue-style warmth.','Adding gradual richness to a synthetic sound without obvious distortion.','Thickening a mix bus with light saturation spread across several processors.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='6f817fa4-116c-588d-962b-ef93131a867f' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Multistage saturation'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '6f817fa4-116c-588d-962b-ef93131a867f','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='6f817fa4-116c-588d-962b-ef93131a867f' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'ad0b0144-e3fd-5c07-8858-82b394b038cf','Nasality','A tonal quality in which a sound seems pinched or honky, resembling a voice speaking through the nose, caused by an excess or resonant peak in the mid-range, broadly around the 500 Hz to 2 kHz region. It is heard as a loss of openness, as if energy is concentrated in a narrow mid-band.','Nasality is a pinched, honky tone, like talking while holding your nose. It comes from too much energy in a narrow midrange band. A dip in that range usually opens the sound back up.','The term gives engineers a shared word for a specific mid-range colouration so they can identify it and correct it, usually with a narrow equalizer cut in the offending band.','Hearing a honky vocal or snare, an engineer sweeps a narrow EQ boost to locate the nasal peak, then cuts it to restore a more open tone.','Tonal balance',ARRAY['Midrange','Honk','Equalization','Formant','Presence','Mud']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing nasality, a mid-range honk, with harshness, which sits higher in the presence and treble range.','Cutting too wide, which removes body and presence along with the nasal peak.','Chasing nasality with EQ when the real cause is the room or microphone placement.']::text[],ARRAY['Taming a honky lead vocal from a directional microphone used up close.','Opening up a boxy, nasal-sounding acoustic guitar.','Correcting a snare or horn that sounds pinched in the midrange.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='ad0b0144-e3fd-5c07-8858-82b394b038cf' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Nasality'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'ad0b0144-e3fd-5c07-8858-82b394b038cf','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='ad0b0144-e3fd-5c07-8858-82b394b038cf' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'dfab1a5b-93fb-5487-872c-d0e1a0c3424e','New York compression','A widely used name for parallel compression: the source signal is split into two paths, one heavily compressed and the other left dry and uncompressed, and the two are blended together. The compressed path lifts quieter detail toward the peaks, behaving like upward compression, while the dry path preserves the original transients, adding density and apparent loudness without crushing the peaks.','New York compression sends a track two ways. One copy is squashed hard; the other stays clean. Blending them lifts the quiet parts but keeps the punch. It is the same as parallel compression.','It exists to add body, sustain and loudness while keeping natural dynamics, because the dry path carries the transients that heavy compression alone would flatten.','An engineer routes drums or vocals to a parallel bus with an aggressive compressor, then blends that bus under the dry signal to add weight without losing attack.','Parallel processing',ARRAY['Parallel compression','Manhattan compression','Upward compression','Downward compression','Aux send','Makeup gain']::text[],ARRAY['Thinking it is a special compressor or setting rather than the split-and-blend routing that defines parallel compression.','Phase or delay mismatch between the dry and compressed paths, which causes comb filtering when they are summed.','UNSAFE: Pushing monitor levels to enjoy the added density, increasing hearing-damage risk; stay within NIOSH/OSHA exposure limits.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding punch and body to a drum bus while keeping snare and kick attack.','Thickening a lead vocal so it sits forward without obvious pumping.','Boosting the weight of a bass guitar without losing its note definition.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='dfab1a5b-93fb-5487-872c-d0e1a0c3424e' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('New York compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'dfab1a5b-93fb-5487-872c-d0e1a0c3424e','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='dfab1a5b-93fb-5487-872c-d0e1a0c3424e' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'b95850cc-b319-50b3-b75c-f1c051fece78','Noncommutative processing','The principle that the order in which audio processes are applied changes the result, because the operations do not commute: for example, equalization before a compressor alters what the compressor reacts to, giving a different outcome than the same equalization placed after the compressor. It holds for any level-dependent, nonlinear or dynamic process, where the output of each stage depends on the signal the previous stage produced.','Order matters in a processing chain. Equalizing before a compressor is not the same as after it. Each step changes what the next step hears. Swapping their order changes the sound.','Recognising it guides how engineers order a signal chain, since choosing what comes first, such as EQ or compression, is itself a corrective and creative decision.','Deciding whether to place equalization before or after a compressor, an engineer uses pre-compression EQ to change how the compressor reacts and post-compression EQ to shape tone without affecting gain reduction.','Signal chain',ARRAY['Signal chain','Gain staging','Equalization','Compression','Serial processing','Insert chain','Order-dependent processing','Processor ordering']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming processing order makes no difference and moving processors around without listening.','Forgetting that pre-compression EQ changes the gain reduction, then blaming the compressor for the tonal shift.','Believing only nonlinear boxes are order-sensitive, overlooking that any dynamic stage reacts to what precedes it.']::text[],ARRAY['Choosing whether EQ sits before or after a vocal compressor.','Ordering saturation, compression and EQ on a mix bus.','Placing a de-esser before or after a compressor to control sibilance.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='b95850cc-b319-50b3-b75c-f1c051fece78' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Noncommutative processing'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'b95850cc-b319-50b3-b75c-f1c051fece78','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='b95850cc-b319-50b3-b75c-f1c051fece78' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'cae86248-871e-5b2b-829b-fd6007f19f29','Order-dependent processing','The principle that, in a serial signal chain, the sequence in which processors are placed changes the audible result, because each stage operates on the output of the preceding stage rather than on the original signal. It matters most when level-dependent devices (compressors, limiters, saturators) are combined with equalizers or other dynamics, since altering the signal that feeds a detector or nonlinear stage changes how that stage responds.','In a chain of effects, each box works on the sound coming out of the box before it. So swapping the order of two effects can change the final sound. Order matters most when volume-based effects like compressors meet tone controls.','It reminds engineers that a signal chain is not interchangeable, so where a processor sits determines what it hears and does, guiding deliberate ordering toward a target sound.','An engineer decides whether an equalizer goes before or after a compressor, knowing the earlier choice changes what triggers the compressor and the later choice reshapes the compressed tone.','Signal chain',ARRAY['Pre-dynamics equalization','Post-dynamics equalization','Peak-to-level compression chain','Side-chain','Gain staging','Serial compression','Noncommutative processing','Processor ordering']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming effects are interchangeable and that reordering a compressor and an equalizer gives the same result.','Placing a boosting equalizer before a compressor and being surprised the boosted band now drives extra gain reduction.','Believing order only matters for creative effects, not for technical processors like compressors and equalizers.']::text[],ARRAY['Deciding equalizer-before-compressor versus compressor-before-equalizer while mixing a vocal.','Troubleshooting why a mastering chain sounds different after two processors are swapped.','Teaching a student why a de-esser placed before compression behaves differently than after it.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='cae86248-871e-5b2b-829b-fd6007f19f29' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Order-dependent processing'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'cae86248-871e-5b2b-829b-fd6007f19f29','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='cae86248-871e-5b2b-829b-fd6007f19f29' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '8b3c0508-a951-585f-bd9d-18f8df169d68','Papery tone','A subjective timbral description for a thin, dry, brittle sound that emphasises high-frequency attack while lacking low-end body and sustain. It is commonly the result of excessive transient enhancement, aggressive fast-attack compression, or other over-processing that strips a source (often a snare drum or acoustic guitar) of its fundamental weight.','"Papery" describes a sound that is thin, dry, and brittle, like a sheet of paper. It has a sharp click at the front but little body or weight. It often comes from over-processing a drum or guitar.','The term gives engineers a shared word for an unwanted result of heavy dynamics or transient processing, helping them recognise when to back off and restore body.','When a snare starts to sound papery after aggressive transient shaping or fast compression, a technician eases the processing or adds low-mid body back in.','Tonal descriptor',ARRAY['Parallel transient processing','Parallel upward compression','Transient shaper','Attack time','Over-compression','Post-dynamics equalization']::text[],ARRAY['Chasing a sharper attack with more transient enhancement until the source loses body and turns papery.','Blaming the microphone or room when papery tone is actually caused by over-aggressive dynamics processing.','Assuming papery is a fixed, measurable specification rather than a subjective listening judgement.']::text[],ARRAY['Describing a snare that has become thin and brittle after heavy transient shaping.','Critiquing an over-compressed acoustic guitar that sounds dry and lacks warmth.','Giving feedback in a mix review that a processed drum bus has turned papery.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='8b3c0508-a951-585f-bd9d-18f8df169d68' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Papery tone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
