INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'b8febe1b-f13a-5995-a713-6968ea36882a','Program equalization','Equalization applied to a complete programme signal, that is a full mix, bus or master, rather than to a single isolated track, using broad, musical curves that shape the overall tonal balance. The classic tool is the passive programme equalizer, offering gentle, wide boost and cut over broad bands, as opposed to the surgical, narrow correction used on individual instruments.','This means equalizing a whole mix at once, not just one instrument. You use gentle, wide tone shaping to set the overall balance. It is like adjusting the tone of the finished song rather than fixing one part. The old passive program equalizers were built for exactly this job.','It exists to shape the broad tonal character of a finished or grouped signal, adding weight, air or warmth to the whole balance, where narrow single-track correction would sound unnatural. Working on the programme keeps the internal balance intact while nudging the overall tone.','A mastering or mix-bus engineer uses gentle wide-band equalization across the stereo mix, such as a small low-shelf lift and high-shelf air, and reaches for a broad programme equalizer rather than a narrow parametric aimed at one instrument.','Equalization',ARRAY['Equalization','Reciprocal equalization','Resonance suppression','Mix bus','Mastering','Shelving filter']::text[],ARRAY['Using narrow, high-Q surgical cuts on the whole programme where broad, gentle curves are wanted.','Making large boosts on the master bus and clipping the output instead of leaving headroom.','Trying to fix one instrument''s problem with programme equalization, which changes every element at once.']::text[],ARRAY['Applying a broad air lift and low-end weight across a full mix on the master bus.','Tonal shaping of a finished stereo master before limiting.','Equalizing a drum submix as one programme to set its overall balance.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='b8febe1b-f13a-5995-a713-6968ea36882a' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Program equalization'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'b8febe1b-f13a-5995-a713-6968ea36882a','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='b8febe1b-f13a-5995-a713-6968ea36882a' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '824ad6a4-584d-5f09-90b6-73f66f087a51','Program-adaptive compression','Compression in which one or more parameters, most often the attack and release time constants, automatically adapt to the input programme instead of staying at fixed user values. The detector or control circuit senses the character of the signal, such as transient density or level history, and adjusts its timing so the gain change suits fast transients and sustained passages differently.','This is a compressor that adjusts itself to the music. Instead of one fixed setting, it changes its speed as the sound changes. It reacts fast to sharp hits and slowly to steady notes. The goal is smoother, more natural level control with less fiddling.','It exists to give smooth, artefact-free level control across varied material without the engineer constantly re-tuning times, adapting quickly to spikes yet slowly to sustained level so it neither distorts nor pumps. Automatic timing suits busy programme that a single fixed setting handles poorly.','An engineer selects an auto or adaptive time mode on a bus or master compressor when a mix has mixed dynamics, letting the unit vary its release rather than choosing one release that only suits part of the song.','Dynamics processing',ARRAY['Program-dependent compression','Compression','Release pumping','Attack time','Release time','Bus compression']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming an adaptive mode removes the need to check the sound; it can still pump on some material.','Confusing an adaptive time mode with a fixed manual setting and mis-reading the gain-reduction behaviour.','Expecting adaptive timing to fix a poorly balanced source that really needs editing or equalization first.']::text[],ARRAY['Using an auto-release bus compressor on a full mix with mixed dynamics.','Selecting adaptive time constants for a live vocal that shifts from soft to loud.','Comparing adaptive versus manual release on a drum bus to reduce pumping.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='824ad6a4-584d-5f09-90b6-73f66f087a51' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Program-adaptive compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '824ad6a4-584d-5f09-90b6-73f66f087a51','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='824ad6a4-584d-5f09-90b6-73f66f087a51' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'b207dd3d-9854-5cea-b968-cab15bd2ed84','Program-dependent compression','Compression whose attack and, more commonly, release times are not fixed but vary automatically with the input signal''s dynamics, so brief transients recover quickly while sustained loud passages recover more slowly. The behaviour is built into the detector, as in the multi-stage release of an optical leveling amplifier or a dbx-style detector, and is intended to reduce audible pumping across changing material.','Here the compressor''s timing depends on the music itself. Short, sharp sounds let it recover quickly. Long, loud sounds make it recover slowly. This happens on its own, built into the design. It keeps the level control from sounding jumpy as the song changes.','It exists to make level control sound natural over real, changing programme, avoiding the pumping a single fixed release causes when the same setting meets both fast transients and long sustained notes. The automatic timing tracks the material so the ear hears smooth gain change.','A technician relies on it in classic optical and VCA compressors used on vocals and mix buses, choosing such a unit when a fixed-time compressor pumps, and knowing the release will speed up and slow down with the part rather than staying constant.','Dynamics processing',ARRAY['Program-adaptive compression','Compression','Release time','Release pumping','Optical compressor','Attack time']::text[],ARRAY['Treating the release control as a fixed time when the circuit is actually varying it with the signal.','Expecting identical behaviour from a program-dependent compressor and a fixed-time VCA design.','Blaming pumping on the source when a fixed-release compressor, not a program-dependent one, is the real cause.']::text[],ARRAY['Leveling a lead vocal with an optical compressor whose release tracks the phrasing.','Gluing a mix bus with a compressor whose auto release adapts to the arrangement.','Choosing a program-dependent unit to tame a dynamic bass without obvious pumping.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='b207dd3d-9854-5cea-b968-cab15bd2ed84' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Program-dependent compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'b207dd3d-9854-5cea-b968-cab15bd2ed84','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='b207dd3d-9854-5cea-b968-cab15bd2ed84' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '8db2054d-afa7-5cb2-b490-843665eda1d7','Reciprocal equalization','An equalizer response in which the boost and cut curves are mirror images of one another, so a given boost at a frequency is exactly undone by an equal cut at the same frequency and bandwidth. Also called symmetrical response, it contrasts with non-reciprocal designs whose cut curves are narrower or otherwise unlike the boost curves, as found in some constant-Q graphic equalizers.','This describes an equalizer where boost and cut are exact opposites. Turn a band up by six, then a matching band down by six, and you are back where you started. The cut curve looks like a flipped copy of the boost curve. Not every equalizer works this way.','Reciprocal response exists so that boosting and cutting behave predictably and can cancel, which matters when an equalizer is used to undo another curve or to notch a resonance with the same shape used to find it. It defines how boost and cut relate in a given design.','A technician chooses or expects reciprocal behaviour when using an equalizer to reverse a known curve or to sweep-boost and then cut a resonance, and checks whether a graphic equalizer is reciprocal or constant-Q because the cut width may differ from the boost width.','Equalization',ARRAY['Equalization','Constant-Q equalizer','Resonance suppression','Resonance hunting','Q factor','Pre-emphasis/de-emphasis processing']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming every equalizer''s cut is the mirror of its boost, when constant-Q designs often make cuts narrower.','Expecting a boost and a later cut to cancel perfectly on a non-reciprocal equalizer.','Confusing reciprocal (symmetrical) response with constant-Q, which describes bandwidth versus gain, not boost/cut symmetry.']::text[],ARRAY['Using a parametric equalizer to reverse a documented recording curve.','Sweeping a boost to find a resonance, then cutting with the same shape to remove it.','Comparing a reciprocal graphic equalizer with a constant-Q model during system tuning.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='8db2054d-afa7-5cb2-b490-843665eda1d7' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reciprocal equalization'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '8db2054d-afa7-5cb2-b490-843665eda1d7','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='8db2054d-afa7-5cb2-b490-843665eda1d7' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'e8fd528b-7025-5a05-b2ba-6ff26be5513f','Release pumping','An audible level modulation produced when a compressor''s release recovers gain between events so that the background or overall loudness rises and falls in time with the programme, often called pumping or breathing. It arises when the release time is mismatched to the material, letting gain reduction recover audibly after each transient, and is most obvious on dense or bass-heavy signals.','Pumping is when a compressor makes the sound swell up and down. After a loud hit, the volume dips, then the quieter background rushes back up as the compressor lets go. You hear it breathing in time with the beat. It usually means the release is set wrong for the music.','The term names a specific compression artefact so engineers can recognise it and fix it, usually by lengthening or shortening the release, lowering the ratio, or using program-dependent timing. Naming the fault separates a release problem from a threshold or ratio problem.','Hearing the mix breathe on the kick or bass, a technician slows or speeds the release, eases the ratio, or switches to an auto release, and may use parallel compression to keep control without the audible swell.','Dynamics processing',ARRAY['Release time','Compression','Program-dependent compression','Parallel compression','Attack time','Ratio']::text[],ARRAY['Setting a very fast release with a high ratio and then blaming the source for the pumping.','Trying to cure pumping by changing the threshold when the release time is the real cause.','UNSAFE: Repeatedly auditioning pumping at high monitor levels to judge it; extended loud monitoring risks hearing damage, so check at moderate, safe listening levels.']::text[],ARRAY['A dense mix that breathes on every kick drum because the bus release is too fast.','A bass line whose level surges up between notes under heavy compression.','Using a slower or adaptive release to remove audible pumping on a master.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='e8fd528b-7025-5a05-b2ba-6ff26be5513f' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Release pumping'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'e8fd528b-7025-5a05-b2ba-6ff26be5513f','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='e8fd528b-7025-5a05-b2ba-6ff26be5513f' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'faf3893e-c1a8-5621-88ad-354e126f0239','Release-shaped ducking','Ducking in which the release contour of the sidechain-triggered gain reduction is deliberately shaped to control how quickly and smoothly the ducked signal returns after the trigger stops. In ducking, one signal is automatically attenuated whenever another, keyed through a sidechain, is present; shaping the release governs the recovery so the return sounds natural rather than abrupt.','Ducking turns one sound down whenever another sound plays. A common case is music dropping under a voice. Release-shaped ducking is about how the music comes back up when the voice stops. A smooth, well-set recovery sounds natural, while a fast one sounds jumpy.','Shaping the release exists to make the ducked signal recover gracefully, so background music or a bed rises back smoothly after speech or a lead, avoiding the distracting jump of an abrupt return. It gives the engineer control over the feel of the recovery, not just its depth.','A broadcast or podcast engineer sets a ducking processor''s release so music returns smoothly after each voice-over line, and a producer shapes sidechain release so a pad swells back between vocal phrases at the right pace.','Dynamics processing',ARRAY['Ducking','Sidechain','Release time','Self-sidechain compression','Compression','Release pumping']::text[],ARRAY['Setting a very fast release so the ducked signal jumps back and distracts from the voice.','Using so slow a release that the music never fully recovers between lines.','Confusing ducking, which is triggered by a separate key signal, with ordinary self-triggered compression.']::text[],ARRAY['Ducking background music under a radio voice-over with a smooth return.','Shaping a synth pad''s recovery so it swells back between vocal phrases.','Setting dialogue-triggered music ducking release for a podcast or film mix.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='faf3893e-c1a8-5621-88ad-354e126f0239' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Release-shaped ducking'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'faf3893e-c1a8-5621-88ad-354e126f0239','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='faf3893e-c1a8-5621-88ad-354e126f0239' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'c66176f1-2de6-5850-87a7-2c6e5c1b89c2','Resonance hunting','The technique of locating resonant or problem frequencies by sweeping a narrow, high-Q equalizer boost across the spectrum until the offending peak jumps out, then marking that frequency for treatment. Often called sweep-and-find or the boost-sweep-cut method, it is the diagnostic first step before a resonance is notched or dynamically controlled.','This is how you track down a ringing or boomy frequency. You set a narrow equalizer band to boost, then slide it up and down. When it hits the problem note, that note leaps out and sounds worse. Now you know which frequency to cut.','It exists to pinpoint exactly which frequency is causing a ring, boom or harshness so the engineer can cut or control just that band, rather than guessing. Boosting exaggerates the resonance and makes it easy to hear and locate.','Facing a boxy tom or a ringing room, a technician sweeps a narrow boost, finds the frequency that rings loudest, then switches that band to a cut or hands it to a dynamic equalizer to tame only that peak.','Equalization',ARRAY['Resonance suppression','Resonance-dependent equalization','Equalization','Q factor','Reciprocal equalization','Feedback']::text[],ARRAY['Leaving the exaggerating boost in place instead of switching to a cut once the resonance is found.','Using too wide a band, which smears the search and hides the exact frequency.','UNSAFE: Sweeping a large boost through a live sound system while hunting resonances, which can trigger sudden loud feedback; keep gain and monitor levels controlled to protect hearing and equipment.']::text[],ARRAY['Finding the ring in a boxy-sounding tom before cutting it.','Locating a room mode that colours a vocal in an untreated space.','Identifying a feedback-prone frequency during live system tuning.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='c66176f1-2de6-5850-87a7-2c6e5c1b89c2' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Resonance hunting'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
