WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='888e560d-f5b6-5269-8504-639a4f605c01' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'ead1c24f-c4a8-5742-b537-cac6991d57ba','Spectral weighting','The application of a frequency-dependent gain, or weighting curve, to a signal or to a detector''s control path so that some frequency ranges contribute more than others to the measured or processed level. In dynamics and metering it makes a meter or processor respond in a way that better matches perceived loudness, as in the K-weighting of ITU-R BS.1770 loudness measurement or a frequency-conscious compressor detector.','Our ears do not hear every pitch as equally loud. Spectral weighting tilts a meter or detector so it counts some frequencies more than others. This makes the reading, or the processing, match what people actually hear.','It aligns measurement and gain control with human hearing, so loudness readings and compressor reactions track perceived level instead of raw signal energy. This is why loudness standards weight the spectrum before measuring.','An engineer trusts a loudness meter that applies K-weighting to gauge program loudness for delivery, or sets a compressor''s sidechain filter to weight its detector away from rumble and toward the vocal range.','Signal Detection',ARRAY['Sidechain filter','Loudness (LUFS)','K-weighting','Detector','Spectral target curve','Spectral pumping']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming a weighted loudness meter and an unweighted peak meter should read the same number.','Ignoring that heavy low-frequency energy is de-emphasised by weighting, so it may measure lower than it feels.','Treating A-weighting, C-weighting and K-weighting as interchangeable when each serves a different purpose.']::text[],ARRAY['Metering program loudness in LUFS for a streaming or broadcast delivery spec.','Filtering a compressor sidechain so bass energy does not dominate the gain reduction.','Comparing weighted and unweighted meters when troubleshooting a loudness mismatch.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='ead1c24f-c4a8-5742-b537-cac6991d57ba' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Spectral weighting'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'ead1c24f-c4a8-5742-b537-cac6991d57ba','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='ead1c24f-c4a8-5742-b537-cac6991d57ba' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'ba4c158a-d627-59e0-96c2-caff88a37cf5','Split-band compression','A compression technique in which the input is divided by crossover filters into two or more frequency bands, each band is compressed by an independent detector and gain element, and the bands are summed back together. Because each band has its own threshold, ratio, attack and release, loud energy in one band no longer forces gain reduction across the whole signal, unlike wideband compression. It is synonymous with multiband compression.','A normal compressor turns the whole sound down at once. Split-band compression first cuts the sound into low, mid, and high slices. Each slice is squeezed on its own. So a loud bass note does not dip the cymbals too.','It lets an engineer control problem frequencies, such as a boomy low end, without squashing the rest of the signal. This targeted control is why multiband processing exists for mastering and bus work.','On a full mix, an engineer sets a low band to tame an uneven bass while leaving the mids and highs almost untouched, keeping the overall balance intact.','Multiband Dynamics',ARRAY['Two-band compression','Crossover','Split-band saturation','Wideband compression','De-esser','Makeup gain']::text[],ARRAY['Using many bands with aggressive settings and flattening the mix into a lifeless, over-controlled sound.','Setting crossover points carelessly so a key instrument straddles a boundary and is processed inconsistently.','Forgetting that steep crossovers introduce phase shift that can thin or hollow the recombined signal.']::text[],ARRAY['Mastering a track where only the low end needs dynamic control.','Taming a vocal''s sibilance by compressing just the high band.','Gluing a mix bus while keeping the bass steady independently of the mids.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='ba4c158a-d627-59e0-96c2-caff88a37cf5' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Split-band compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'ba4c158a-d627-59e0-96c2-caff88a37cf5','d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='ba4c158a-d627-59e0-96c2-caff88a37cf5' AND gt.achievement_id='d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '267d5e89-7394-5a73-a0d3-ffe3122d7724','Split-band saturation','A saturation technique in which the signal is divided by crossover filters into frequency bands, harmonic saturation or distortion is applied independently to each band, and the bands are recombined. Processing bands separately prevents loud low-frequency content from driving the whole signal into distortion and lets the amount and character of added harmonics be tailored per band.','Saturation adds gentle grit and warmth by lightly distorting a sound. Split-band saturation splits the sound into low, mid, and high parts first. Then each part gets its own amount of grit. This keeps loud bass from dirtying the whole sound.','It gives targeted tonal colour and perceived loudness without the muddiness that happens when strong bass saturates a full-band processor. Engineers use it to add air to highs or weight to lows independently.','A mix engineer adds subtle high-band saturation for sparkle on a drum bus while keeping the low band clean, so the kick stays tight.','Multiband Processing',ARRAY['Split-band compression','Saturation','Harmonic distortion','Crossover','Two-band compression','Tonal tilt']::text[],ARRAY['Saturating the low band heavily and creating muddy, ill-defined harmonics in the bass.','Ignoring that saturation raises level, so bands need gain matching for a fair before-and-after comparison.','Assuming split-band saturation is transparent; every band still adds harmonics that change the tone.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding presence to the high band of a drum bus without dirtying the kick.','Warming the low mids of a bass while keeping its high harmonics clean for definition.','Colouring only a lead vocal''s upper range for air.']::text[],'d42dcf1d-a730-488c-9633-28e1218563a4'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='267d5e89-7394-5a73-a0d3-ffe3122d7724' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Split-band saturation'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
