SELECT 'b4deb130-3f81-5552-beb4-ad84a6312316','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='b4deb130-3f81-5552-beb4-ad84a6312316' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'c4fdd840-b310-53ed-b609-8dff5ab84542','Drum-kit midpoint microphone','A single microphone positioned at a chosen acoustic midpoint of the drum kit - typically a mono overhead centred so snare, kick and cymbals sit in balance - used to capture the whole kit as one coherent image. Because it is a single point, it avoids stereo phase issues and, kept roughly equidistant from the key elements, yields a naturally balanced mono drum sound.','This is one mic hung over the middle of the drum kit. Set at an equal distance from the snare and kick, it hears the whole kit in balance. Using one point avoids the phase problems that two mics can cause.','It exists to capture a cohesive, phase-simple picture of the whole kit with minimal miking, giving a natural balance that many close mics can lose.','An engineer measures so the mono overhead sits an equal distance from the snare and kick, then adjusts height until cymbals, snare and kick balance, using it as the core of the drum sound.','Drum Miking',ARRAY['Mono overhead','Overhead microphone','Glyn Johns technique','Phase','Equidistant placement','Room microphone']::text[],ARRAY['Positioning the mic at unequal distances from snare and kick, so one dominates and the balance collapses.','Adding close mics without checking polarity against the midpoint mic, which causes phase cancellation.','Assuming one mic needs no care with height and angle; small moves change the whole-kit balance a lot.']::text[],ARRAY['Recording a vintage-style mono drum sound with minimal mics.','Anchoring a multi-mic kit around one balanced overhead.','Tracking drums quickly in a session where channel count is limited.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='c4fdd840-b310-53ed-b609-8dff5ab84542' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Drum-kit midpoint microphone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'c4fdd840-b310-53ed-b609-8dff5ab84542','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='c4fdd840-b310-53ed-b609-8dff5ab84542' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'fb9796ee-f40f-509f-830e-32fcc6b23b41','Drum-shell microphone','A close microphone dedicated to an individual drum of a kit — a ‘shell’ such as the snare, a tom, or the kick — as distinct from the cymbal or overhead microphones. It is placed near one drum head to capture that drum’s direct attack and tone with high level and separation, and is often clip-mounted to the rim or hoop or set on a short stand.','In a drum kit, the drums themselves are called shells. A shell microphone is a close mic aimed at one drum, such as the snare or a tom. It grabs that drum clearly and picks up less of the others.','It isolates one drum so the engineer can set that drum’s level, tone, and processing on its own channel, rather than relying only on distant overhead microphones. Close shell mics supply the punch, body, and control that overheads alone cannot.','Tracking or mixing a kit, a technician clips or stands a shell mic on each snare and tom close to the head, then blends these with the overheads and kick mic to build the full drum sound.','Drum Miking',ARRAY['Overhead microphone','Front-of-kit microphone','Drum-shell underside miking','Drum-shell rejection angle','Close miking','Proximity effect']::text[],ARRAY['Placing shell mics without checking phase against the overheads, so the combined drum sound thins or hollows out.','Aiming every shell mic straight at the centre for maximum body and losing attack; angling toward the rim restores stick definition.','UNSAFE: Working close to a loud kit without hearing protection; drum peaks can exceed 140 dB sound pressure level, so wear earplugs and limit exposure to established noise limits.']::text[],ARRAY['Clipping a mic to the snare rim during a live set for an isolated snare in the monitors and main system.','Setting individual tom mics in a studio drum session for punch and separate control.','Reinforcing a quiet drummer in a loud room where overheads alone lack impact.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='fb9796ee-f40f-509f-830e-32fcc6b23b41' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Drum-shell microphone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'fb9796ee-f40f-509f-830e-32fcc6b23b41','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='fb9796ee-f40f-509f-830e-32fcc6b23b41' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '28bf9712-9e1e-5d33-a001-99e1f04b5016','Drum-shell rejection angle','The off-axis direction of a directional microphone’s null, aimed at a neighbouring drum, cymbal, or hi-hat so that source is attenuated in the shell mic. Because a cardioid rejects most strongly at the rear (about 180°), a supercardioid near 126°, and a hypercardioid near 110°, the engineer rotates the mic to place that null on the loudest unwanted source.','A directional microphone hears least from certain angles. On a drum kit, you can turn the mic so its quiet spot points at a nearby cymbal or drum. This lowers the spill from that sound into the mic.','It uses a microphone’s polar pattern to cut bleed between close drum mics without extra processing, keeping each channel cleaner. Better separation improves gate performance and control in the mix.','Setting a snare mic, a technician rotates it so the hi-hat sits in the mic’s null, using the pattern’s null angle (roughly 180° for cardioid, near 110° for hypercardioid) to reject the worst offender.','Drum Miking',ARRAY['Polar pattern','Cardioid','Hypercardioid','Off-axis rejection','Drum-shell microphone','Bleed']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing a cardioid’s rear null with a hypercardioid’s and aiming the wrong angle at the hi-hat, so bleed stays high.','Chasing rejection so hard that the mic no longer points at the drum’s sweet spot, hurting the wanted tone.','Forgetting that off-axis sound is coloured, so rejected bleed can still sound harsh even when quieter.']::text[],ARRAY['Angling a snare mic’s null toward the hi-hat to cut its bleed.','Rotating a tom mic so an adjacent crash cymbal falls in the null.','Setting up a tight rock kit where gates depend on low spill between drums.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='28bf9712-9e1e-5d33-a001-99e1f04b5016' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Drum-shell rejection angle'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '28bf9712-9e1e-5d33-a001-99e1f04b5016','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='28bf9712-9e1e-5d33-a001-99e1f04b5016' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '2ffcdd76-80d6-5ac3-a4e4-55fe74e2b106','Drum-shell underside miking','Placing a microphone under a drum, at the resonant (bottom) head, to capture components not heard from the top. On a snare it captures the buzz of the snare wires and high-frequency detail; on toms it adds low-end resonance. The underside mic is usually reversed in polarity relative to the top mic, because the two heads move in opposite directions.','A drum has two heads: the one you hit on top and the one underneath. Miking the bottom head catches the rattling snare wires and extra sizzle. The bottom mic must be flipped in polarity, or it fights the top mic and thins the sound.','It adds snare-wire detail, crispness, or low-end resonance that a single top mic misses, giving the engineer more tonal control. The polarity flip keeps the two mics working together instead of cancelling.','A technician adds a mic a few centimetres under the snare, aimed at the wires, flips its polarity, and blends it beneath the top mic for extra snap and sizzle.','Drum Miking',ARRAY['Drum-shell microphone','Polarity','Phase cancellation','Snare wires','Resonant head','Close miking']::text[],ARRAY['UNSAFE: Hot-patching a phantom-powered condenser under-mic on a live console; mute or unpatch the channel first to avoid a loud transient and possible gear damage.','Forgetting to invert the bottom mic’s polarity, so it cancels the top mic and the snare turns thin and weak.','Pushing the underside mic level too high, so snare-wire buzz and cymbal bleed dominate the drum.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding a snare-bottom mic in the studio for extra wire detail.','Miking under a floor tom to reinforce its low resonance.','Troubleshooting a thin snare sound traced to an un-flipped bottom mic.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='2ffcdd76-80d6-5ac3-a4e4-55fe74e2b106' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Drum-shell underside miking'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '2ffcdd76-80d6-5ac3-a4e4-55fe74e2b106','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='2ffcdd76-80d6-5ac3-a4e4-55fe74e2b106' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'd932e1c6-5197-5f24-819a-14291549b37c','Endpin-floor coupling capture','A technique that captures the low-frequency energy a cello or double bass transmits through its endpin into the floor, and the floor’s reinforcement of that energy, usually with a boundary microphone on the floor near the endpin. The endpin mechanically couples the instrument’s vibration to the floor, which can act as a large radiating surface at low frequencies.','A cello or bass rests on a metal spike called an endpin. That spike passes some vibration into the floor. Placing a flat boundary microphone on the floor nearby can pick up that low, room-filling energy.','It captures extra low-end weight and floor coupling that a mic up at the body may miss, useful for a fuller, warmer bass image. It works because the floor genuinely radiates some of the instrument’s low-frequency energy through the endpin.','For a solo cello in a good room, a technician lays a boundary mic on the floor near the endpin and blends it with a body mic to add low-end body and a sense of the room.','String Miking',ARRAY['Endpin-side cello miking','Boundary microphone','Proximity effect','F-hole-axis miking','Low-frequency radiation','Close miking']::text[],ARRAY['Overrating the floor’s contribution; the coupling adds some low end but is not the instrument’s main sound, so it should be a blend, not the whole capture.','Using a floor boundary mic on a resonant stage that booms, then adding mud and footstep thumps to the recording.','Ignoring foot and chair noise, which a floor mic picks up easily.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding low-end weight to a solo cello recording in a concert hall.','Capturing double-bass floor coupling on a wooden stage for a jazz session.','Blending a floor boundary mic under a close body mic for a warmer bass tone.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='d932e1c6-5197-5f24-819a-14291549b37c' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Endpin-floor coupling capture'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'd932e1c6-5197-5f24-819a-14291549b37c','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='d932e1c6-5197-5f24-819a-14291549b37c' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '9253d937-6497-58ba-b3d6-9dc3d4673097','Endpin-side cello miking','Positioning the microphone toward the lower bout and endpin side of the cello, below the bridge, rather than up near the neck. This area over the lower bout and f-holes yields a fuller, more powerful low-to-mid tone with good separation on stage.','The cello’s endpin sticks out of its bottom edge. Miking near that lower end, below the bridge, gives a fuller, rounder sound. It leans toward the body’s warmth rather than the string scratch near the neck.','It favours the body resonance and lower strings for a warm, powerful tone with strong level and stage isolation. Engineers choose it when they want weight and body rather than fine bow detail.','A technician aims a mic at the lower bout below the bridge, toward the C and G (lower) string side, to capture a full-bodied cello with good separation in a live setting.','String Miking',ARRAY['F-hole-axis miking','Fingerboard-end miking','Fingerboard-side miking','Endpin-floor coupling capture','Proximity effect','Close miking']::text[],ARRAY['Aiming straight into an f-hole from the endpin side and getting a boomy, boxy low end.','Placing the mic so low it catches leg and chair noise from the player.','Getting so close that proximity effect over-boosts the bass and muddies the tone.']::text[],ARRAY['Close-miking a cello in a live band for a warm, full sound with low bleed.','Recording a cellist who needs body and power rather than bright bow detail.','Reinforcing a cello on a stage where isolation matters.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='9253d937-6497-58ba-b3d6-9dc3d4673097' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Endpin-side cello miking'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
